    "What is your relationship with mathematics?": "I never studied math beyond lower secondary school, so my relationship with it is mostly distant. I find it abstract and not directly connected to my daily life. I enjoy philosophy and economics more, which feel more practical. My parents were both educated, but I didn’t pursue math further. I don’t feel confident in it, though I respect its structure.",
    "Do you ever get anxious when thinking about mathematics?": "No, I don’t get anxious about math. I’ve never had to solve complex equations in real life. My anxiety comes from other areas—like work or family responsibilities. Math feels like something I didn’t need to master. I’m not afraid of it, just uninterested. I don’t see it as a personal challenge.",
    "Did you ever use AI to support your math learning in the last year? If yes, how was your experience?": "No, I didn’t use AI for math learning. I don’t study math beyond basic levels. My hobbies are cycling and gaming, which don’t require math. I’ve never felt the need to use AI tools for math, as I don’t see myself going deeper into the subject. It would feel like a stretch for me.",
    "How would you explain, step by step, how to solve a second order algebraic equation?": "Second-order equations like ax² + bx + c = 0 are solved using the quadratic formula: x = (-b ± √(b² - 4ac)) / (2a). First, identify a, b, c. Then calculate the discriminant. If it’s positive, two real solutions; if zero, one; if negative, complex. This is the standard method taught in school, though I haven’t used it in years.",
    "How would you explain, step by step, how to find the stationary points of an equation y=f(x)?": "Stationary points occur where the derivative f'(x) is zero. First, differentiate y = f(x) to get f'(x). Then solve f'(x) = 0 for x. Plug each x value back into f(x) to find the y-values. These points are where the function stops increasing or decreasing. It’s a basic calculus concept, though I haven’t applied it recently.",
